          "question": "I can't breathe properly and my inhaler isn't working. My chest feels really tight.",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now (for example, 911 in the US or 999 in the UK). Do not wait.**\n\nSevere difficulty breathing is an emergency. Sit upright, and if you have your own asthma action plan, use the emergency reliever medicine it describes while help is on the way. (Source: *Severe difficulty breathing*, emergency medicine and respiratory society guidance.)\n\nThe same guidance says that breathlessness at rest, or breathlessness that comes on suddenly, needs emergency care. It also says that in an asthma attack, if there is no improvement after reliever treatment, that is an emergency.\n\nChest tightness together with breathlessness can also be a sign of a heart problem. The chest pain guidance says to treat this as a possible heart attack until proven otherwise. (Source: *Chest pain that may indicate a heart attack*.) Do not wait to see whether it passes.\n\nIf someone is with you, ask them to stay with you and to call for help. Do not drive yourself to hospital.\n\nI can only share what these guidance documents say. They cannot tell me what is causing your symptoms, and this is not a substitute for emergency assessment.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
